{"date": "2026-11-03", "post_id": 1507042, "title": "Comet PanSTARRs and Rainbow Meteor", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-3-comet-panstarrs-and-rainbow-meteor/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>Did this meteor come from that comet? Probably not. Although many\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/facts/\">meteors</a>\u00a0do originate from comets, there are\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_periodic_comets\">thousands</a>\u00a0of\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/\">comets</a>\u00a0that have visited the inner\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/\">Solar System</a>\u00a0recently, and matching meteors to them can be tricky. And to be clear, the\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DXcM2fhDcSA/\">pictured comet</a>\u00a0is far behind the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-november-27-the-meteor-and-the-comet/\">streaking meteor</a>. Comet\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-14-the-long-wispy-tail-of-comet-r3-panstarrs/\">C/2025 R3 (PanSTARRS)</a>\u00a0was captured from\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/iGdY96A0TUE\">Hautacam</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France\">France</a>\u00a0in April when the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-january-4-comet-halleys-nucleus-an-orbiting-iceberg/\">giant space iceberg</a>\u00a0sported impressive\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-october-6-the-changing-ion-tail-of-comet-lemmon/\">ion</a>\u00a0(blue) and dust\u00a0<a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/comets/en/anatomy-of-a-comet.en.jpg\">tails</a>. Sand-sized rocky debris that enters\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-is-earths-atmosphere/\">Earth\u2019s atmosphere</a>\u00a0typically appears as\u00a0<a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sb/meteor_streams.html\">meteors</a>, while the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-24-chemicals-glow-as-a-meteor-disintegrates/\">colorful foreground streak</a>\u00a0here may indicate a complicated composition. This month three\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.amsmeteors.org/calendar/\">notable meteor showers</a>\u00a0will reach their peaks. Over the next few days, the Southern\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/watch-the-skies/2015/10/26/the-taurid-swarm-is-upon-us/\">Taurids</a>\u00a0will be reaching their apex, while the Northern\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurids\">Taurids</a>\u00a0will top out about a week later. Both originate from\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2003-december-23-comet-encke-returns/\">Comet 2P/Encke</a>. November is most famous for the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/leonids/\">Leonids Meteor Shower</a>, though, which originates from\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2002-november-16-tempel-tuttle-the-leonid-comet/\">Comet 55P/Tempel-Tuttle</a>\u00a0and will\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/WhatsWrongWithYourCat/comments/1xioc6j/cat_wont_sleep_in_his_bed_chooses_the_weirdest/#lightbox\">reach its crescendo</a>\u00a0in about two weeks.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>a hidden gem", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/eddy_metais_photo/\">Eddy Metais</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/eddy_metais_photo/\">Eddy Metais</a>", "alt": "A comet with a long flowing tail is seen below a star-filled  sky and above a peaked hill. Starting right in the tail of the comet is a multicolored meteor. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-3-comet-panstarrs-and-rainbow-meteor/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/CometMeteor_Metais_1934.jpg?w=0&h=0&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 3 - Comet PanSTARRs and Rainbow Meteor</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-3-comet-panstarrs-and-rainbow-meteor/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation:\u00a0Did this meteor come from that comet? Probably not. Although many\u00a0meteors\u00a0do originate from comets, there are\u00a0thousands\u00a0of\u00a0comets\u00a0that have visited the inner\u00a0Solar System\u00a0recently, and matching meteors to them can be tricky. And to be clear, the\u00a0pictured comet\u00a0is far behind the\u00a0streaking meteor. Comet\u00a0C/2025 R3 (PanSTARRS)\u00a0was captured from\u00a0Hautacam,\u00a0France\u00a0in April when the\u00a0giant space iceberg\u00a0sported impressive\u00a0ion\u00a0(blue) and dust\u00a0tails. Sand-sized rocky debris that enters\u00a0Earth\u2019s atmosphere\u00a0typically appears as\u00a0meteors, while the\u00a0colorful foreground streak\u00a0here may indicate a complicated composition. This month three\u00a0notable meteor showers\u00a0will reach their peaks. Over the next few days, the Southern\u00a0Taurids\u00a0will be reaching their apex, while the Northern\u00a0Taurids\u00a0will top out about a week later. Both originate from\u00a0Comet 2P/Encke. November is most famous for the\u00a0Leonids Meteor Shower, though, which originates from\u00a0Comet 55P/Tempel-Tuttle\u00a0and will\u00a0reach its crescendo\u00a0in about two weeks.Tomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0a hidden gem\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"comet panstarrs and rainbow meteor\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Comet PanSTARRs and Rainbow Meteor\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-3-comet-panstarrs-and-rainbow-meteor/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/CometMeteor_Metais_1934.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/CometMeteor_Metais_1934.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A comet with a long flowing tail is seen below a star-filled  sky and above a peaked hill. Starting right in the tail of the comet is a multicolored meteor. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-03T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-03T08:34:13-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Comet PanSTARRs and Rainbow Meteor\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/CometMeteor_Metais_1934.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 3<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/CometMeteor_Metais_1934.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/CometMeteor_Metais_1934.jpg\" alt=\"A comet with a long flowing tail is seen below a star-filled  sky and above a peaked hill. Starting right in the tail of the comet is a multicolored meteor. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Comet PanSTARRs and Rainbow Meteor </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/eddy_metais_photo/\">Eddy Metais</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>Did this meteor come from that comet? Probably not. Although many\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/facts/\">meteors</a>\u00a0do originate from comets, there are\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_periodic_comets\">thousands</a>\u00a0of\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/\">comets</a>\u00a0that have visited the inner\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/\">Solar System</a>\u00a0recently, and matching meteors to them can be tricky. And to be clear, the\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DXcM2fhDcSA/\">pictured comet</a>\u00a0is far behind the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-november-27-the-meteor-and-the-comet/\">streaking meteor</a>. Comet\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-14-the-long-wispy-tail-of-comet-r3-panstarrs/\">C/2025 R3 (PanSTARRS)</a>\u00a0was captured from\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/iGdY96A0TUE\">Hautacam</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France\">France</a>\u00a0in April when the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-january-4-comet-halleys-nucleus-an-orbiting-iceberg/\">giant space iceberg</a>\u00a0sported impressive\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-october-6-the-changing-ion-tail-of-comet-lemmon/\">ion</a>\u00a0(blue) and dust\u00a0<a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/comets/en/anatomy-of-a-comet.en.jpg\">tails</a>. Sand-sized rocky debris that enters\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-is-earths-atmosphere/\">Earth\u2019s atmosphere</a>\u00a0typically appears as\u00a0<a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sb/meteor_streams.html\">meteors</a>, while the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-24-chemicals-glow-as-a-meteor-disintegrates/\">colorful foreground streak</a>\u00a0here may indicate a complicated composition. This month three\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.amsmeteors.org/calendar/\">notable meteor showers</a>\u00a0will reach their peaks. Over the next few days, the Southern\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/watch-the-skies/2015/10/26/the-taurid-swarm-is-upon-us/\">Taurids</a>\u00a0will be reaching their apex, while the Northern\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurids\">Taurids</a>\u00a0will top out about a week later. Both originate from\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2003-december-23-comet-encke-returns/\">Comet 2P/Encke</a>. November is most famous for the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/leonids/\">Leonids Meteor Shower</a>, though, which originates from\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2002-november-16-tempel-tuttle-the-leonid-comet/\">Comet 55P/Tempel-Tuttle</a>\u00a0and will\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/WhatsWrongWithYourCat/comments/1xioc6j/cat_wont_sleep_in_his_bed_chooses_the_weirdest/#lightbox\">reach its crescendo</a>\u00a0in about two weeks.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>a hidden gem\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-2-a-long-cloud-on-mars/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261103/html"}